The warning "Non-atomic operation on volatile field 'modCount'" indicates that the `modCount` field is being modified in a non-atomic manner, which could lead to visibility issues in a multi-threaded environment. The `volatile` keyword ensures visibility of changes across threads but does not guarantee atomicity.

To address this issue, you should ensure that all modifications to `modCount` are done atomically. In your case, since `modCount` is incremented by one each time it's modified, you can use an `AtomicInteger` instead of a regular integer. This will provide both visibility and atomicity guarantees.

Here's how you can modify your code:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

// ...

private AtomicInteger modCount = new AtomicInteger();

// ...

public void pushFirst(E elem) {
    expandIfNeeded();
    front = (front - 1 + array.length) % array.length;
    array[front] = elem;
    size++;
    modCount.incrementAndGet(); // Atomic increment
}

public void pushLast(E elem) {
    expandIfNeeded();
    array[rear] = elem;
    rear = (rear + 1) % array.length;
    size++;
    modCount.incrementAndGet(); // Atomic increment
}

public E popFirst() {
    if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
    E elem = array[front];
    array[front] = null; // Allow GC to work
    front = (front + 1) % array.length;
    size--;
    modCount.incrementAndGet(); // Atomic increment
    shrinkIfNeeded();
    return elem;
}

public E popLast() {
    if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
    rear = (rear - 1 + array.length) % array.length;
    E elem = array[rear];
    array[rear] = null; // Allow GC to work
    size--;
    modCount.incrementAndGet(); // Atomic increment
    shrinkIfNeeded();
    return elem;
}

public void clear() {
    Arrays.fill(array, null);
    front = 0;
    rear = 0;
    size = 0;
    modCount.incrementAndGet(); // Atomic increment
}
```

By using `AtomicInteger`, you ensure that every modification to `modCount` is performed atomically, eliminating the need for explicit synchronization or other mechanisms to handle concurrent access safely.

