Here's an example of how you can configure Access Control Lists (ACLs) in Tailscale for your specified requirements:

1. Create a policy file named `tailscale-acl.conf` with the following content:

```conf
# Allow everything in the 'nill' group to access all servers of all groups on all ports.
policy "nill" {
    allow = [
        { src: "nill", dst: "*", port: "*" },
    ]
}

# Allow members of the 'family' group to use any server from any group as an exit-node,
# but do not grant them access to any services on the network servers.
policy "family" {
    allow = [
        { src: "family", dst: "*", port: "exit-node" },
    ]
    deny = [
        { src: "family", dst: "*", port: "*" },
    ]
}

# Grant the 'servers' group access to 22/tcp, 80/tcp, and 443/tcp to all servers of all groups,
# while denying access to all other ports and protocols.
policy "servers" {
    allow = [
        { src: "servers", dst: "*", port: [22, 80, 443] },
    ]
    deny = [
        { src: "servers", dst: "*", port: "*" },
    ]
}
```

2. Apply this configuration by running the following command on each node where you want to enforce these rules:

```bash
sudo tailscale up --authkey=<your-auth-key> --acl-config-file=/path/to/tailscale-acl.conf
```

Replace `<your-auth-key>` with your actual Tailscale authentication key and `/path/to/tailscale-acl.conf` with the path to the `tailscale-acl.conf` file.

This setup will ensure that nodes belonging to the 'nill' group have unrestricted access, nodes in the 'family' group can only be used as exit-nodes without accessing services directly, and nodes in the 'servers' group can only connect via SSH (port 22), HTTP (port 80), or HTTPS (port 443). All other connections will be denied for the 'servers' group.

