```matlab
% Clear workspace and close all figures
clear;
close all;

% Define parameters
L = 0.42; % Length of the tank (m)
W = 0.36; % Width of the tank (m)
U = 2.5;  % Wind speed (m/s)

% Create grid for spatial discretization
x = linspace(0, L, 100);
y = linspace(0, W, 100);
[X, Y] = meshgrid(x, y);

% Initialize velocity field with zero initial conditions
u = zeros(size(X));
v = zeros(size(Y));

% Time-stepping loop
dt = 0.01; % Time step size (s)
t_final = 10; % Final time (s)
num_steps = round(t_final / dt);

for t = 1:num_steps
    % Update velocities using simple advection equation
    u_new = u - U * dt ./ max(abs(u), eps) .* sign(u);
    v_new = v;

    % Apply boundary conditions (no-slip at walls)
    u_new(:, end) = 0;
    u_new(end, :) = 0;
    v_new(:, end) = 0;
    v_new(end, :) = 0;

    % Update fields
    u = u_new;
    v = v_new;

    % Plotting
    figure;
    quiver(X, Y, u, v);
    title(['Time: ', num2str(t*dt)]);
    xlabel('X Position (m)');
    ylabel('Y Position (m)');
    axis([0 L 0 W]);
    drawnow;
end

% End of script
```

This code sets up a simple numerical model where the wind causes horizontal motion (`u`) along the x-axis while keeping vertical motion (`v`) constant initially. It uses a forward Euler method for updating the velocity components based on their current values and applies no-slip boundary conditions at the edges of the tank. The `quiver` function plots the resulting flow pattern at each time step.

Please note that this is a very simplified model and does not account for many physical phenomena such as surface tension, viscosity effects, or nonlinear interactions between different parts of the wave system. For more accurate simulations, consider using specialized software like OpenFOAM or ANSYS Fluent which have built-in solvers designed specifically for complex fluid dynamics problems.

