Creating a Sierpinski Triangle using XAML can be done by defining a custom control or using a Canvas to draw the fractal iteratively. Below is an example of how you might create a simple Sierpinski Triangle using WPF (Windows Presentation Foundation) and XAML.

First, let's define a `SierpinskiTriangle` class that will handle the drawing logic:

```csharp
using System.Windows;
using System.Windows.Controls;
using System.Windows.Media;

public class SierpinskiTriangle : Control
{
    public static readonly DependencyProperty DepthProperty =
        DependencyProperty.Register("Depth", typeof(int), typeof(SierpinskiTriangle), new PropertyMetadata(0));

    public int Depth
    {
        get { return (int)GetValue(DepthProperty); }
        set { SetValue(DepthProperty, value); }
    }

    protected override void OnRender(DrawingContext drawingContext)
    {
        base.OnRender(drawingContext);

        if (Depth < 1) return;

        double width = ActualWidth;
        double height = ActualHeight;

        Point p1 = new Point(0, height);
        Point p2 = new Point(width / 2, 0);
        Point p3 = new Point(width, height);

        DrawTriangle(drawingContext, p1, p2, p3, Depth);
    }

    private void DrawTriangle(DrawingContext dc, Point p1, Point p2, Point p3, int depth)
    {
        if (depth == 0) return;

        // Calculate midpoints
        Point m1 = MidPoint(p1, p2);
        Point m2 = MidPoint(p2, p3);
        Point m3 = MidPoint(p3, p1);

        // Draw smaller triangles
        dc.DrawGeometry(Brushes.Black, null, CreateTriangle(p1, m1, m3));
        dc.DrawGeometry(Brushes.Black, null, CreateTriangle(m1, p2, m2));
        dc.DrawGeometry(Brushes.Black, null, CreateTriangle(m3, m2, p3));

        // Recursively draw smaller triangles
        DrawTriangle(dc, p1, m1, m3, depth - 1);
        DrawTriangle(dc, m1, p2, m2, depth - 1);
        DrawTriangle(dc, m3, m2, p3, depth - 1);
    }

    private Point MidPoint(Point p1, Point p2)
    {
        return new Point((p1.X + p2.X) / 2, (p1.Y + p2.Y) / 2);
    }

    private Geometry CreateTriangle(Point p1, Point p2, Point p3)
    {
        PathFigure figure = new PathFigure();
        figure.StartPoint = p1;
        figure.Segments.Add(new LineSegment(p2, true));
        figure.Segments.Add(new LineSegment(p3, true));
        figure.Segments.Add(new LineSegment(p1, true));

        PathGeometry geometry = new PathGeometry();
        geometry.Figures.Add(figure);

        return geometry;
    }
}
```

Next, you need to declare this custom control in your XAML file:

```xml
<Window x:Class="YourNamespace.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:local="clr-namespace:YourNamespace"
        Title="MainWindow" Height="450" Width="800">
    <Grid>
        <local:SierpinskiTriangle Depth="5" />
    </Grid>
</Window>
```

In this example:
- The `SierpinskiTriangle` class handles the rendering of the Sierpinski Triangle.
- The `Depth` property controls the level of recursion for the fractal.
- The `OnRender` method is overridden to perform the actual drawing.
- The `DrawTriangle` method recursively draws smaller triangles until the specified depth is reached.

Make sure to replace `"YourNamespace"` with the appropriate namespace where your `SierpinskiTriangle` class is defined. This should give you a basic implementation of a Sierpinski Triangle in WPF using XAML.

To send a POST request with a client certificate in C#, you can use the `HttpClient` class along with the `SslClientAuthenticationOptions` class to specify the client certificate. Below is an example of how to do this:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Path to your client certificate file (e.g., .pfx or .cer)
        string certPath = "path/to/your/certificate.pfx";
        // Password for the certificate if it's encrypted
        string certPassword = "your_certificate_password";

        // Create a new HttpClient instance
        using (HttpClient client = new HttpClient())
        {
            // Load the client certificate
            X509Certificate2 clientCert = new X509Certificate2(certPath, certPassword);

            // Set up SSL/TLS options to include the client certificate
            var handler = new HttpClientHandler();
            handler.ClientCertificates.Add(clientCert);
            handler.SslProtocols = SslProtocols.Tls12; // Ensure TLS 1.2 or higher

            // Use the custom handler with the HttpClient
            client = new HttpClient(handler);

            // Define the URL and content for the POST request
            string url = "https://example.com/api/resource";
            var content = new StringContent("{\"key\":\"value\"}", System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

            try
            {
                // Send the POST request
                HttpResponseMessage response = await client.PostAsync(url, content);

                // Check the response status code
                if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
                {
                    // Read the response content
                    string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
                    Console.WriteLine("Response: " + responseBody);
                }
                else
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"Error: {response.StatusCode}");
                }
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine("An error occurred: " + ex.Message);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
1. **Client Certificate**: The `X509Certificate2` class is used to load the client certificate from a `.pfx` or `.cer` file. If the certificate is password-protected, you need to provide the password.

2. **HttpClientHandler**: The `HttpClientHandler` is configured to include the client certificate using the `ClientCertificates` property. This ensures that the certificate is sent with the request.

3. **POST Request**: The `PostAsync` method is used to send the POST request. The content of the request is defined as a JSON string wrapped in a `StringContent` object.

4. **SSL/TLS Configuration**: The `SslProtocols` property is set to `Tls12` to ensure that the connection uses at least TLS 1.2, which is recommended for security.

5. **Error Handling**: The program includes basic error handling to catch and display any exceptions that occur during the request.

### Notes:
- Replace `"path/to/your/certificate.pfx"` and `"your_certificate_password"` with the actual path to your certificate file and its password.
- Replace `"https://example.com/api/resource"` with the actual URL you want to send the POST request to.
- Ensure that the server you are sending the request to is configured to accept client certificates.

This code should work in most environments where you need to send a POST request with a client certificate in C#.

To extract hashtags from a given text in C#, you can use regular expressions (regex). Here's an example of how to do it:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string inputText = "This is a sample text with #hashtags and @mentions like #example1 and #example2.";

        // Define the regex pattern for matching hashtags
        string hashtagPattern = @"#\w+";

        // Use Regex.Matches to find all matches in the input text
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(inputText, hashtagPattern);

        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Hashtags:");
        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
        }
    }
}
```

In this code:
- We define a `hashtagPattern` that matches any word starting with a hash symbol (`#`) followed by one or more word characters (`\w+`).
- The `Regex.Matches` method finds all occurrences of the pattern in the input text.
- Finally, we iterate through the matches and print each hashtag.

When you run this program with the provided `inputText`, it will output:

```
Extracted Hashtags:
#hashtags
#example1
#example2
```

This approach ensures that only valid hashtags are extracted from the text.

